Familial Hypercholesterolaemia
Cardiovascular
Premature coronary artery disease, Achilles tendon xanthomas, Corneal arcus in youth, Severely elevated LDL cholesterol

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CDV-016
Reason for Visit: Acute onset of severe, exertional chest tightness in a young adult with a known family history of early heart attacks.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a young, otherwise fit adult experiencing acute, exertional chest tightness that has only partially resolved with rest. Given the stated family history of sudden cardiac death in their 30s, this presentation raises an immediate, high-probability red flag for premature acute coronary syndrome. Urgent physical assessment and electrocardiography were mandatory to rule out active myocardial ischemia and could not be safely delayed until the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I was just carrying groceries and got this crushing weight on my chest, and it terrifies me because my dad died of a heart attack at my age."
History of Present Illness: Patient (aged 34) reports a new onset of recurrent angina pectoris over the last two weeks, culminating in a severe episode of central chest tightness today while carrying groceries. The pain radiated to the left jaw and was associated with mild to severe generalized fatigue. Symptoms partially subsided after 20 minutes of sitting but a dull ache remains. The patient notes a strong family history of markedly elevated cholesterol and premature myocardial infarction. KEY SYMPTOMS: Recurrent angina pectoris. Mild to severe generalized fatigue if ischaemic heart disease develops. Family history of markedly elevated cholesterol or premature myocardial infarction. Premature onset of coronary artery disease before the age of 55 in men.
Functional Status: Normally highly active, currently resting and deeply anxious.
Social/Safety Concerns: High psychological distress regarding family history of premature mortality.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 18 | SpO2: 98 percent | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Anxious, slightly pale, but conversing normally.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Regular rate and rhythm, normal S1 and S2, no murmurs. No signs of heart failure. Extremities: Firm, nodular swellings palpated bilaterally in the Achilles tendons (Tendon xanthomas). HEENT: Yellowish cholesterol deposits noted on the medial aspect of both upper eyelids (Xanthelasmas). A greyish-white ring is visible at the periphery of the cornea bilaterally (Corneal arcus). DIAGNOSTIC TETRAD: Premature coronary artery disease (suspected clinically), Achilles tendon xanthomas, Corneal arcus in youth, Severely elevated LDL cholesterol (pending formal laboratory confirmation).
Environmental Observations: Patient’s gym bag and healthy meal prep visible, contrasting with the high intrinsic cardiac risk.
Medication Reconciliation: Not currently on any lipid-lowering therapy.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Familial Hypercholesterolaemia (Presenting with premature angina) - Status: Exacerbated (High Risk)
Differentials:

Musculoskeletal Chest Pain - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Pain was explicitly exertional, radiating to the jaw, and set against a profound background of physical stigmata for severe hyperlipidemia (xanthomas, arcus) and family history.

Gastroesophageal Reflux - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Onset tied directly to physical exertion rather than meals or supine positioning.

Panic Attack - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Although anxious, the classic exertional nature of the pain and the objective presence of tendon xanthomas point definitively to an organic ischemic etiology.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with classic exertional angina at an unusually young age, accompanied by pathognomonic physical signs of severe lipid storage disease (Achilles xanthomas, corneal arcus) and a critical family history of premature cardiac death. This clinical picture is highly indicative of undiagnosed Familial Hypercholesterolaemia presenting as premature coronary artery disease. The current episode of rest pain/prolonged angina suggests unstable plaque. Deferring assessment until routine hours posed an imminent threat of acute myocardial infarction or sudden cardiac death, strictly justifying the urgent home visit and immediate escalation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Aspirin 300mg orally stat. Arranged immediate transfer to the Emergency Department via ambulance for continuous ECG monitoring, troponin levels, and urgent cardiology assessment for probable acute coronary syndrome.
Positive Behavioral Change: Instructed the patient to remain strictly seated and practice deep, slow breathing to physically lower their heart rate. By intentionally controlling their breathing, the patient reduces the oxygen demand on their heart muscle. The reward is a decrease in the residual chest ache and a sense of control during a terrifying event.
Home-Based Services: None.
DME/Safety: None.
Follow-up: Acute hospital admission expected. Specialist lipid clinic referral required post-discharge.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance dispatched. If the crushing chest pain returns fully, or if the patient loses consciousness, the spouse is instructed to commence CPR immediately if they become unresponsive.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis of the underlying genetic condition requires formal blood tests and genetic screening; acute coronary syndrome is the presumptive immediate threat.
Clinical Course: Familial Hypercholesterolaemia causes extremely rapid plaque buildup. Lifelong, aggressive cholesterol-lowering medication is essential to prevent recurrent heart attacks.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to walk or drive. Chew the aspirin provided. Wait for the paramedics to arrive.

